(Database Connection Pooling Every connection to a relational database carries real cost.) Tj T*
(Opening a TCP socket, completing authentication, and negotiating a session on the server side) Tj T*
(takes tens of milliseconds, and doing that for every query would make an application unusably) Tj T*
(slow under any real load. Connection pooling solves this by keeping a set of already) Tj T*
(established connections open and handing them out to application threads on demand, then) Tj T*
(returning them to the pool instead of closing them when the work is done. HikariCP is the) Tj T*
(default connection pool in Spring Boot applications and is widely regarded as the fastest and) Tj T*
(most reliable option on the JVM. It achieves its speed through aggressive bytecode-level) Tj T*
(optimization, minimal locking, and by avoiding unnecessary wrapper allocations around the) Tj T*
(underlying JDBC driver objects. A HikariCP pool is configured primarily through a small number) Tj T*
(of properties: maximum pool size, minimum idle connections, connection timeout, and max) Tj T*
(lifetime. Sizing a connection pool correctly is less intuitive than it looks. A common mistake) Tj T*
(is assuming that a larger pool always improves throughput. In practice, once the pool size) Tj T*
(exceeds the number of CPU cores available to the database server, additional connections mostly) Tj T*
(increase contention rather than parallelism, because the database itself becomes the) Tj T*
(bottleneck. A widely cited formula for sizing is connections equal to core count times two,) Tj T*
(plus the number of effective spindles, though this should always be validated against real load) Tj T*
(testing rather than applied blindly. Pool exhaustion is one of the most common production) Tj T*
(incidents involving connection pools. It happens when every connection in the pool is checked) Tj T*
(out and none are returned quickly enough, so new requests queue up waiting for a connection) Tj T*
(timeout. The usual causes are long running transactions holding a connection open, connections) Tj T*
(leaked because a try-with-resources block was not used, or a downstream dependency slowing down) Tj T*
(every query enough that the pool cannot keep up with demand. Monitoring pool utilization, wait) Tj T*
(time, and connection lifetime is essential to catching this before it becomes an outage. Max) Tj T*
(lifetime deserves particular attention. Database servers, load balancers, and firewalls) Tj T*
(frequently close idle or long lived connections without warning the client. Setting max) Tj T*
(lifetime slightly shorter than any such server side timeout ensures the pool proactively) Tj T*
(retires connections before they are killed out from under an in flight query, which otherwise) Tj T*
(surfaces as a confusing and intermittent connection reset error deep inside application code.) Tj T*
